          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Sometimes for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            having some fixed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+property"} extra property
            and/or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+structure"} extra structure
          %p
            There are several typical classes of such
            %em reconstruction theorems
            (which are all to some extent related).
          %h2#tannakian_reconstruction_theorems Tannakian reconstruction theorems
          %p
            These theorems reconstruct an algebraic symmetry object (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"}> group
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gerbe"} gerbe
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebra"} Hopf algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation"} representation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric
            or
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"} braided
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+representations"} category of representations
            is called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannaka+duality"} Tannaka duality
          %p
            Examples include the classical Tannaka theorem and Krein theorem,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Doplicher-Roberts+reconstruction+theorem"} Doplicher-Roberts reconstruction theorem
            in physics,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne%27s+theorem+on+tensor+categories"} Deligne's theorem on tensor categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+functor"} fiber functor
            (due to Grothendieck) is central to these considerations.
          %h2#reconstruction_theorems_for_schemes Reconstruction theorems for schemes
          %p
            These theorems for schemes (or varieties only) reconstruct a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scheme"} scheme
            (variety) out of the category of quasicoherent or only coherent sheaves (or a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
            version of them). In that class one can find the Gabriel–Rosenberg theorem, the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bondal-Orlov+reconstruction+theorem"} Bondal-Orlov reconstruction theorem
            %a{:href => "http://www.math.ucla.edu/~balmer"} P. Balmer
            and of
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://www-maths.swan.ac.uk/staff/gg"} G. Garkusha
          %p
            There is a large class of
            = succeed "," do
              %strong abelian reconstruction theorems
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriel-Popescu+theorem"} Gabriel-Popescu theorem
            %em topos theory
            the
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giraud+theorem"} Giraud theorem
            is also a reconstruction theorem (of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            out of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
          %p Examples:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freyd-Mitchell+embedding+theorem"} Freyd-Mitchell embedding theorem
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriel-Popescu+embedding+theorem"} Gabriel-Popescu embedding theorem
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen-Gabriel+embedding+theorem"} Quillen-Gabriel embedding theorem
          %h2#gabrielulmer_duality Gabriel-Ulmer duality
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriel-Ulmer+duality"} Gabriel-Ulmer duality
            is an equivalence of 2-categories LFP of locally finitely presentable categories and Lex of finitely complete categories. It is related to syntax-semantics adjunction and to Tannaka type reconstruction for coalgebra-like objects, with which has a common generalization (enriched Tannaka duality of Day).
          %p
            Gabriel–Ulmer duality has a generalisation to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sound+doctrines"} sound doctrines
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sifted+colimits"} sifted colimits
            rather than
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+colimits"} filtered colimits
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere%27s+reconstruction+theorem"} Lawvere's reconstruction theorem
          %h2#heuristics Heuristics
          %p
            Typically in the proofs of most reconstruction theorems an implicit use of the Yoneda lemma is involved. Various embedding theorems of classes of categories (as well as theorems on realization as quotient categories) are closely related, e.g.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Barr+embedding+theorem"} Barr embedding theorem
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freyd-Mitchell+embedding+theorem"} Freyd-Mitchell embedding theorem
          %p
            Many of these examples are corollaries of the theory of
            = succeed "." do
              %em lex colimits
